# Enterprise OU Scoping Logic (Frontend)

Yes, this is the **Enterprise Standard** way. In large systems (ERP, CRM, RBAC), we don't just check "Can this user see this?"; we check "Does this data fall within the user's **Tree Branch**?".

This document explains how the logic we've implemented in your Angular app actually works to secure data.

---

## 1. The Core Data Structures
To make this work, every User and every Data Object (like a Bill, a Lead, or a sub-User) must have an **OU Path**.

```typescript
interface UserContext {
  id: number;
  roles: string[];                  // Functional Ability (e.g. ['Admin', 'Editor'])
  ouPath: string;                   // Hierarchical Scope (e.g. '/1/2/')
}

interface DataObject {
  id: number;
  organizationUnitPath: string;     // The "Room" it belongs to (e.g. '/1/2/5/')
}
```

---

## 2. The Relationship Logic: "Is Sub-Path"
The magic happen by checking if the **Data's Path** starts with the **User's Path**.

| User Path | Data Path | Result | Logic |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `/1/` (Group) | `/1/2/5/` | **ALLOWED** ✅ | Group Admin can see EVERYTHING below them. |
| `/1/2/` (Titan) | `/1/2/5/` | **ALLOWED** ✅ | Titan Manager can see Sales Team (/5) data. |
| `/1/2/5/` (Sales) | `/1/2/` | **BLOCKED** ❌ | Sales Team cannot see their parent's (Titan) data. |
| `/1/3/` (Motors) | `/1/2/5/` | **BLOCKED** ❌ | Motors cannot see Titan/Sales data. |

---

## 3. Implementation on the Frontend (Angular)

### A. The Permission Directive
We use an Angular Directive to hide buttons if the user doesn't have the **Ability** AND the **Scope**.

```typescript
// Example Usage:
// <button *appIfScope="userContext" [targetPath]="item.path"> Delete </button>

@Directive({ selector: '[appIfScope]' })
export class IfScopeDirective {
  @Input() set appIfScope(user: UserContext) {
    const isSubPath = targetPath.startsWith(user.ouPath);
    const hasRole = user.roles.includes('Admin');
    
    if (isSubPath && hasRole) {
      showElement();
    } else {
      hideElement();
    }
  }
}
```

### B. List Filtering (The "Global Filter" Pattern)
Whenever we call an API (like [getUsers](file:///d:/angular/src/app/pages/services/api/organization.service.ts#83-88)), we automatically append the `OrganizationUnitId` or [Path](file:///d:/angular/src/app/pages/administration/components/organization/organization.component.ts#168-177) to the request.

*   **Request**: `GET /api/users?ouId=2`
*   **Backend**: Automatically adds `WHERE Path LIKE '/1/2/%'` to the SQL.
*   **Result**: The user **never even sees** rows that aren't in their scope.

---

## 4. Why this is the "Enterprise Way"
1.  **Materialized Path Performance**: Using breadcrumb strings (`/1/2/5/`) instead of recursive database joins is extremely fast ($O(1)$ string comparison).
2.  **Scalability**: You can have 10,000 departments and 1 million users, and the check remains just a simple string `startsWith()` operation.
3.  **Visual Safety**: The UI looks "clean" to the user—they only see the branches of the company they are responsible for. It feels like they have their own private version of the app.

---

## 5. Security Note
> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Frontend is for UX, Backend is for Security.**
> While we hide the buttons on the frontend for a great user experience, the **Backend API** must perform the same path check to prevent malicious users from guessing IDs in the URL.
